    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A line of 39 sacred kings, traditionally founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahoeitu">ʻAhoʻeitu</a> around 950, whose royal centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mua-tonga">Muʻa</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongatapu">Tongatapu</a> is marked by great stone tombs.</li><li>Its voyaging and tribute networks reached ʻUvea, Samoa and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, and its royal centres held stone tools from as far as the Society Islands.</li><li>From the fifteenth century junior royal lines governed in its name; the last Tuʻi Tonga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laufilitonga">Laufilitonga</a>, died in 1865 and the title lapsed.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In July 1777 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> watched the ʻinasi first-fruits ceremony at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mua-tonga">Muʻa</a> and wrote that its occasion was the Tuʻi Tonga Paulaho and his son eating together, a thing never done before.</p></aside>
    </section>

